---
title: Demand Forecasting & Inventory Simulation
summary: >-
  Development and Evaluation of a Machine Learning Demand Forcasting System for
  Reducing Food Waste and Stockout Incidents in UK Grocery Retail
featured: true
order: 1
industry: Retail
tools:
  - python
  - power-bi
keyResult: >-
  [Add your heXGBoost forecast daily grocery demand 33% more accurately than a
  simple moving average. In simulation, that meant 30.4% less food waste and
  35.9% fewer stockouts.adline result, e.g. "Best model cut forecast error by X%
  vs ARIMA"]
problem: >
  Large supermarket chains spend a lot of money on forecasting. Many smaller and
  medium sized shops do not. They still decide what to reorder based on recent
  sales averages. This approach is simple and cheap, but it is blind to the
  things that actually move grocery demand, such as the season, promotions, and
  normal week to week changes. Machine learning handles these patterns much
  better, and a large amount of research has shown it produces more accurate
  forecasts than simple averaging.

  But there is a gap in this research. Most studies compare models against each
  other, report accuracy scores, and stop there. They rarely ask the question a
  retailer would actually care about. If the forecast is more accurate, are the
  orders that follow from it actually any better? Accuracy is treated as the end
  result, instead of being treated as one input into a decision. This project is
  built to close that gap for UK grocery retail specifically.


  The problem is can a machine learning demand forecasting system reduce food
  waste and stockout incidents in UK grocery retail, and which forecasting
  approach performs best inside a system like this?
data: >-
  Source: Store Item Demand Forecasting Challenge (Kaggle, 2018). It’s a public
  dataset made for testing forecasting models.


  Size: 913,000 rows of daily sales for 50 products across 10 stores, which is
  500 store-product combinations.


  Time span: five years of daily sales, from 1 January 2013 to 31 December 2017.


  What’s in it: four columns: date, store, item and units sold.


  Cleaning and preparation:


  Dates: I turned the date column into a proper date format and sorted the rows
  by store, item and date, so each product’s sales ran in order.

  Features: I built 18 features from the raw sales:

  lag values, meaning what sold 1, 7 or 365 days earlier

  rolling averages

  calendar details: day of the week, week of the year, month, quarter, year and
  whether it was a weekend

  Dropped rows: the 365-day lag needs a full year of history, so the first year
  of every store-product series had gaps. Removing those gaps dropped 182,500
  rows, which is about 20% of the data. That’s 500 series × 365 days, and it
  left 730,500 rows for modelling.

  Train/test split: I split the data by date, using the first 80% of the
  timeline for training and the last 20% for testing. A random split would have
  let the models “see the future”.


  Limitations of the data: the dataset is synthetic and very regular. It has no
  prices, promotions, weather or holiday effects, so real shop data will be
  noisier.


  Supporting dataset: UCI Online Retail II (Chen, 2012) holds real transactions
  from a UK-based online retailer, covering December 2009 to December 2011. I
  used it as a second test to see how the system copes with real, messy UK
  retail data.
approach: >-
  Compared ARIMA, Random Forest, XGBoost forecasts, fed them into an inventory
  simulation, and built a Flask web app and a Power BI dashboard so
  non-technical users could explore the results.
results: >-
  XGBoost was the best model. Its average forecast error (MAE) was 6.17 units a
  day. The simple moving average, which is close to how many shops order today,
  was off by 9.24 units a day. That makes XGBoost 33.2% more accurate than the
  baseline


  Random Forest came a close second. Both machine learning models did much
  better than the simple average because they learn weekly patterns, seasonal
  trends and recent sales together. An R² of 0.935 means XGBoost explained about
  93.5% of the day-to-day change in sales.


  ARIMA was only run on one product in one store, so its scores can’t be fairly
  compared with the others.


  What it means for stock


  Forecast accuracy only matters if it changes what ends up on the shelf. So I
  ran an inventory simulation that placed orders using each model’s forecasts
  and compared the results against the baseline:


  Waste: surplus stock fell from 834,085 to 580,836 units, which is 30.4% less
  simulated food waste.

  Stockouts: unmet demand fell from 851,730 to 545,935 units, which is 35.9%
  fewer simulated stockouts.


  Better forecasts improved both problems at the same time. Shops usually trade
  one against the other: order more and you waste more, order less and you run
  out. Here the shelves held less of what nobody wanted and more of what people
  came in for
cover: /images/projects/demand-forecasting-and-inventory-simulation/cover.png
githubUrl: https://github.com/testimonyadewale/msc-grocery-forecasting
status: complete
